I ruled on conductor-9, amended the package and committed it. The ruling file is at `/Users/mindlace/Projects/sfhousing/.claude/worktrees/incremental-game/.superpowers/superconductor/incremental-game/rulings/conductor-9.md`

**The ruling:** the finding stands, and the package now follows D8 ("every one is kept as its own saved game"). A delete made on one device no longer throws away changes made on another.
- A copy that is unchanged on another device is still deleted there at the next sync.
- If a device changed the game before it learned of the delete, it keeps its version as its own saved game, under the same name. This holds whether or not that game is the current one.

**What changed, as current-state text:**
- **S2 interface row:** "A 410 keeps the saved game in this browser under a new id and sends it again." Because it is a copied row, the same line changed in the design doc and in the contracts, game-core and game-save specs. That is why `Changes contract` is yes.
- **Design doc, accounts section:** the bullet on deleted games now says a changed copy on another device survives.
- **`game-save` spec:**
  - A 410 rekeys any saved game, not just the current one, and sends it again with `baseRev: null`.
  - The pull rule now says the 410 path keeps the changed save.
  - The browser test now checks both cases.

The store needs no change: `rekey` already handles a game that is not current, and it adds no game, so the 12-game cap doesn't come into play.

**For whoever reads the ruling:**
- `contracts` is listed as `clarification`, since its spec text changed and it is running. No unit has a plan yet, so no branch needs cutting back.
- `game-core`'s copy of the S2 row also changed, but that unit hasn't started, so the ruling file doesn't list it.
- The build-graph entry is `game-save`.
- I skipped the optional Jev citation check.
